+## New cross-avenue mechanism
+
+**NEW: marked-sheet transfer for a cubic block (26+31+30, with 25 as monodromy bookkeeping).** Let
+
+`A=C[f,g]`, `B=B_K subset C[x,y]`, and `A^2 --g1--> Y=Spec(B) --g2--> A^2`
+
+be the accepted sandwich with `d2=3`. BD-GAL makes the cubic extension non-Galois and says every branch divisor has a fixed geometric sheet. At the generic point of an irreducible branch divisor `D`, degree accounting then leaves the only ramified pattern `2R_D+U_D`: one ramified residue-degree-one prime and one unramified residue-degree-one prime. The source image misses `R_D`; over `D` it can meet only the marked unramified component `U_D`.
+
+The proposed mechanism is to push the factorial divisor relation on `A^2` back through the etale quasi-finite first leg and ask whether it makes `[U_D]` torsion or principal in `Cl(Y)`. Since `g2^*D=2R_D+U_D` is principal and the binding class-lattice theorem makes every nonzero divisor supported in `Y-g1(A^2)` have infinite-order class, any valid torsion/principalization of `U_D` would force a forbidden torsion relation on `R_D`.
+
+The gap is explicit: pullback to a UFD through a nonproper quasi-finite map does not automatically descend Cartierness, and boundary correction terms may survive a norm/pushforward. The mechanism succeeds only if the Zariski-Main compactification shows those corrections are supported in the already injected boundary lattice in a way that cannot cancel `R_D`; otherwise it fails cleanly. This is not the closed `d2=2` target, does not assert existence of a block, and does not use the uncharged affine-linear Miranda packets.
